<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="6d3d2f67">&lt;p&gt;
    Basic components of the interview:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Introduction - includes the formal introduction, purpose and expectations
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Issue/opportunity - discussion of the issues and/or opportunities previously developed
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Information gathering - asking key questions to address gaps in the current information
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Closing - follow up actions and next steps up
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    The interview questions should be designed to discover issues to discuss. It represents a way to generate more
    meaningful questions during the other data gathering methods. The interview questions should always include these basic
    inquiries: who, what, when, where, how much, how often and why.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    During the interview, validate the assumptions developed from the data gathering process. The questions determine the
    differences between assumptions and facts in any business process.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;
    Common Pitfalls
&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Asking clients for interviews can be difficult due to the personal nature of the interview. Planning where to meet
        and when can also be difficult. Participants can cancel or change the meeting place at the last minute.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        During the actual interview, critical information can be missed. This can arise from the multitasking that the
        interviewer must do. Not only do they have to make the respondent feel very comfortable, they have to keep as much
        eye contact as possible, write down as much as they can and think of follow up questions.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Documenting the interview can be extremely time consuming. The nature of qualitative research does not lend itself
        well to quantitative analysis. Project team members may report more missing data in interviews than other
        information gathering methods.
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
